# Spelling and Misspelt Words: Writing the Word Right

> Competitive exams lose more marks to spelling than to grammar —
> one silent letter, one doubled consonant, one *-ible/-able* slip,
> and the "error spotting" answer changes. The good news: English
> spelling is more rule-governed than it looks. Learn the rules,
> memorise the rogues, and spelling questions become free marks.

## 1. The i-before-e Rule — and Its Reliable Core

[definition]
The classic rule: **i before e, except after c**, when the sound is
"ee" — *believe, relieve, piece, achieve*; but after *c*:
*receive, ceiling, perceive, conceit*. The rule also bends before
"ay"-sounding vowels: *neighbour, weigh, vein, freight*.
[/definition]

| Pattern | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| i before e (ee sound) | believe, achieve, relief, brief, field, hygiene |
| ei after c (ee sound) | receive, deceive, ceiling, receipt, perceive |
| ei (ay sound) | vein, veil, weigh, eight, reign, freight, neighbour |
| Common exceptions to memorise | seize, weird, protein, caffeine, counterfeit, leisure, either, neither |

*Weird* is weird — that is the mnemonic ("we are weird").

## 2. -able vs --ible, -ant vs --ent

The most-tested pair of suffixes:

- **-able** is the default for English-built words and words with
  intact stems: *comfortable* (comfort), *dependable* (depend),
  *readable*, *acceptable*.
- **-ible** usually attaches to Latin roots with no standalone
  English stem: *audible* (hear), *visible* (see), *plausible*,
  *terrible*, *credible*, *feasible*.

Tip: if you drop the suffix and get a real English word (-able
family does this: laugh → laughable), you almost always want
**-able**.

The same logic splits **-ant/-ance** (claim, defend → claimant,
defendant, relevant, abundant) from **-ent/-ence** (*insistent,
persistent, innocent, existence*) — these are memory pairs to
learn by frequency: relevant, significant, important (a);
independent, existence, persistent (e).

## 3. Doubling and Dropping: The Consonant Rules

1. **One-syllable word, short vowel, one consonant → double before
   -ed/-ing/-er:** stop → stopped/stopping, run → runner, big →
   biggest.
2. **Two-syllable word stressed on the second syllable → double:**
   begin → beginning, refer → referred, permit → permitted; but
   **visit → visited** (stress on first syllable — no doubling).
3. **Silent final e usually drops:** love → loving, argue →
   argued; but **keep the e after soft c/g:** notice → noticeable,
   change → changeable (the e protects the soft sound).
4. **-cede / -ceed / -sede:** only *exceed, proceed, succeed*
   take -ceed; only *supersede* takes -sede; all others take
   -cede (*precede, concede, recede, secede*).

## 4. Plurals That Betray You

| Rule | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| Consonant + y → -ies | baby → babies, duty → duties |
| Vowel + y → -s | key → keys, boy → boys |
| -f/-fe usually → -ves | leaf → leaves, knife → knives (roofs, chiefs, beliefs stay -fs) |
| -o words: musical/Italian origin → -es | tomato → tomatoes, potato → potatoes (photos, pianos stay -s) |
| Irregulars | child → children, man → men, ox → oxen, foot → feet |
| -is → -es (Greek) | analysis → analyses, crisis → crises, basis → bases |
| Unchanged plurals | sheep, deer, aircraft, series, species |

## 5. The Rogues' Gallery: Most Frequently Misspelt

| Correct | Common error | Correct | Common error |
| --- | ---| --- | --- |
| accommodate (two c, two m) | accomodate | embarrass (two r, two s) | embarass |
| necessary (one c, two s) | neccessary | occasion (two c, one s) | occassion |
| definitely | definately | separate | seperate |
| privilege (no d) | privelege | maintenance | maintainance |
| guarantee | gurantee | government | goverment |
| questionnaire (double n) | questionaire | rhythm | rythm |
| conscience | concience | millennium (double l, double n) | millenium |
| occurrence (two c, two r) | occurence | tomorrow (one m, two r) | tommorow |
| liaison | liason | sophomore-style surplus: | — |
| colonel (kernel sound) | — | sergeant | sargeant |

[info title="British vs American"]
Exams in India follow **British spelling**: *colour, honour,
favourite, labour* (-our); *centre, metre, theatre* (-re);
*travelled, cancelled, jewellery* (doubled l); *organise, realise*
(-ise); *defence, licence (noun)/license (verb)*. American texts
flip these: *color, center, traveled, organize, defense, defense
licence-* — stay consistent within one document.
[/info]

## 6. Silent Letters and Pronunciation Traps

Spelling errors cluster where sound gives no clue: **si-lent
letters** in *island* (s), *debt/doubt* (b), *solemn/autumn* (n),
*receipt/psychology* (p), *Wednesday* (first d), *handsome* (d),
*parliament* (i). Then come chameleons — words whose spelling
misled by sound: *pronunciation* (not *pronounciation*),
*arctic* (not *artic*), *February* (not *Febuary*), *library*
(not *libery*), *arithmetic*, *category* (middle e), *committee*.

[example]
Exam-style error spotting: "The comittee decided to recomend seperate
awards for the occassion." Four errors hide here — *comittee*
(committee), *recomend* (recommend), *seperate* (separate),
*occassion* (occasion). Notice every error is in the rogues'
gallery above: spelling questions are memory questions wearing a
grammar costume.
[/example]

## 7. A Strategy That Works

1. **Learn roots, not words:** *cred* (believe) → credible,
   credence, incredible — the -ible follows the Latin root.
2. **Group by trap:** make your own lists of -able/-ible,
   double-letter, and silent-letter words you personally miss.
3. **Say the spelling aloud** for rogues: "ac-com-MO-date",
   "nec-es-SAR-y".
4. **Watch the suffix rules** (doubling, y→i, silent-e) — they are
   mechanical once practised.
5. **Proofread backwards** (last word first) — the brain reads
   whole-word shapes forward and skips its own typos.

[summary]
English spelling runs on rules with a known criminal element:
i-before-e except after c (with *seize, weird, protein* as
memorised rebels); -able for English stems, -ible for rootless
Latin words; -ant/-ent in frequency-learned pairs; doubling rules
keyed to stress (begin → beginning, visit → visited); -cede for
all but *exceed, proceed, succeed* and *supersede*; and plural
families (-ies, -ves, -es, unchanging). The rogues' gallery —
accommodate, embarrass, separate, definite, occasion, millennium —
supplies most exam answers, and British spelling (-our, -re, -ise,
doubled l) is the Indian standard. Learn roots, group traps, say
spellings aloud — and spelling becomes the cheapest section to
score.
[/summary]
